      "ordinal": 3104,
      "eventId": "event://abiogenesis/6d6876d82228eaf61f7adfc9a1ad5325c1e5657efb0e4253f43dd3c2f698e72d",
      "graphFunctionRef": "graph-function://abiogenesis/worksite/native-work@5",
      "cCallRef": "c-call:sha256:598e1a169cf788fc6a90cf64241cf99767fcddc8096864bf735346946a2786f6",
      "resultRef": "result://abiogenesis/a55f6c05d82e61635cb5da84a86e74b336178f6015071e3bfded650abf0aa3f3",
      "contractRef": "contract://abiogenesis/worksite/native-work/observation@5",
      "resultClass": "success",
      "valueKind": "native_workspace_work_observation",
      "changedPaths": [
        "generated/hello-world.mjs"
      ],
      "summary": "I fixed the CLI output: `generated/hello-world.mjs` now prints `Hello, world!` followed by one newline. My own check shows it working, but that isn't independent acceptance.\n\n**Before the fix.** The admitted baseline run was against the original CLI (`sha256:647697a5…4993`), which contained `console.log(\"Hello world!\");`. It printed `Hello world!\\n` (13 bytes, no comma) and exited 0. The supplied test command `node --test test/component/hello-cli.test.mjs test/uat/hello-cli.uat.test.mjs` exited 1: pass 0, fail 2. Both tests failed with an exact-match error: expected `'Hello, world!\\n'`, got `'Hello world!\\n'`.\n\n**The change.** I edited one line in the only file I was allowed to write, adding the missing comma: `console.log(\"Hello, world!\");`. The new file hash is `sha256:fe8f4080…e9ef`. The fix still uses only Node built-ins, with no package or network dependency.\n\n**My check after the fix.**\n- `node generated/hello-world.mjs` printed exactly `Hello, world!\\n` (14 bytes, confirmed with a byte dump) and exited 0.\n- The test command reported tests 2, pass 2, fail 0 and exited 0.\n\n**Protected files are unchanged.** I compared hashes after the edit, and they match the admitted values:\n- original task: `ce602ce2…`\n- oracle: `028073e4…`\n- witness contract: `f97359de…`\n- component test: `8b55bf3f…`\n- UAT test: `5449c227…`\n\nThe baseline failure is still the record for the old file's hash; it was not reassigned to the new file. I did not create any other files."
    },
